- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).
- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).
- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.

Key insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects
that actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="cb2d8a2c" lines="13-26">
<description>
Extend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.

Input structure:
- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.
- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.

Rule:
- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.
- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.
- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.

Key insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="409aa875" lines="68-81">
<description>
Fire projectiles from 3-pixel triangle sprites; mark collisions with maroon or blue.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Non-bg 3-pixel sprites shaped as axis-aligned L's or diagonal V's.

Rule:
- Each triangle has an apex and an emission direction determined by its shape: L-shapes fire diagonally away from the missing corner; V-shapes fire along the axis away from the open side.
- A projectile lands exactly 5 cells from the apex in the emission direction.
- If it hits an existing sprite, that entire sprite is recolored to maroon (9).
- If two projectiles land on the same empty cell, that cell becomes blue (1); a lone projectile on empty bg paints maroon (9).

Key insight: The triangle's orientation encodes both direction and target; dual-projectile collisions on open bg produce blue, not maroon.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="4a21e3da" lines="8-20">
<description>
Cut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.

Input structure:
- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).

Rule:
- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).
- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.
- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.

Key insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="e376de54" lines="41-55">
<description>
Align parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.

Rule:
- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.
- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.
- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.
- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.

Key insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="7c66cb00" lines="9-24">
<description>
Dock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).
- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.

Rule:
- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.
- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.
- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.
- Erase original sprites from the top area.

Key insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="88e364bc" lines="102-115">
<description>
Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.
- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.

Rule:
- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.
- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.
- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.

Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="78332cb0" lines="10-23">
<description>
Reorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).
- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.

Rule:
- Two blocks "connect" when their facing edges share foreground pixels at the same offset (e.g., A's right edge ∩ B's left edge).
- Chain all blocks in the orientation (horizontal or vertical) that admits a complete sequence.
- Output is a 1×N strip of blocks in chain order, separated by sep lines.

Key insight: Edge-contact overlap determines both block ordering and output orientation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="faa9f03d" lines="36-52">
<description>
Repair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.

Input structure:
- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.
- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.
- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.
- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.

Rule:
- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.
- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.
- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.
- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.

Key insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="6e453dd6" lines="39-52">
<description>
Slide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.

Input structure:
- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.
- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).

Rule:
- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.
- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.
- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.

Key insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as "nozzles" projecting rightward.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="3a25b0d8" lines="9-22">
<description>
Fill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.
- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.

Rule:
- Find the two 8-connected objects.
- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).
- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.

Key insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="136b0064" lines="25-39">
<description>
Draw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.

Input structure:
- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.
- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.

Rule:
- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.
- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).
- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.
- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.

Key insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="5545f144" lines="191-207">
<description>
Move a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.

Input structure:
- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.
- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.

Rule:
- Start from the rightmost frame.
- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable
  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the
  nearest one.
- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.
- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.

Key insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="15" source="eval" puzzle_id="65b59efc" lines="9-24">
<description>
Decode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.

codex
实现方案定下来了：

1. `main()` 做真实几何追踪：先识别灰色管道、边缘入口信号、彩色 3×3 空心框出口，再按“当前来向 + 邻接管道数 + T 口旁橙标相对左右”逐步模拟。
2. `generate_input()` 不会随便长网络，而是随机拼接若干条互不冲突的“走廊式”路径片段，强制混入直行、L 弯、T 口左转、T 口右转四类情况，并随机化尺寸、信号数、颜色和出口位置。
3. 写完后直接跑题目给的 100 次自检和 `py_compile`，如果有退化样例或识别漏洞就当场修。
